  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How can insect muscles beat so fast?",
      "a": "In bees, flies and beetles the flight muscles are a special kind that contract again automatically when they are stretched. The springy thorax stretches each set in turn, so one nerve signal can produce many beats.",
      "asks": [
        "Why are insect wingbeats so fast?",
        "How do flies flap so quickly?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is resilin?",
      "a": "Resilin is a rubbery protein in insect wing hinges and joints. It is one of the springiest materials known, giving back nearly all the energy put into stretching it. Fleas use it to power their jumps too.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes insect wings springy?",
        "What protein helps fleas jump?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do insects buzz?",
      "a": "The buzz is the sound of the wings beating the air. The faster the wingbeat, the higher the pitch, so a mosquito's whine is higher than a bee's hum.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes the buzzing sound?",
        "Why does a mosquito whine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How fast does a bee flap its wings?",
      "a": "A honeybee beats its wings about 230 times a second. It uses short, quick strokes rather than big sweeps, which is unusual for an insect its size.",
      "asks": [
        "How many times a second do bee wings beat?",
        "How do bees fly?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do butterflies fly with slow wingbeats?",
      "a": "Butterflies have large, light wings and flap only about 5 to 12 times a second. Their muscles fire once for each beat, like ours, giving a fluttering, bobbing flight that makes them hard for birds to catch.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do butterflies flutter?",
        "How slowly do butterflies flap?"
      ]
    }
  ],
